/**
 * Wringer delivery refusal
 *
 * Generated from `schema/refusal.schema.json`. Do not edit.
 */
export type Refusal = {
  "schema_version": "wringer.refusal.v1";
  /** WHICH refusal this was, as a name a machine can switch on. The closed list of 23 lives in `deliver.REFUSAL_REASONS` and is deliberately NOT duplicated as an enum here. Two reasons, and the second is the load-bearing one. (1) A public tuple is a strictly better guard, because a derived test can read it: `tests/test_refusal.py` walks `ast.parse(deliver.py)` for every `raise Refused(` and asserts set equality with the tuple in BOTH directions, so a site with no name and a name no site raises are each red immediately — an enum copied into this file could go stale while the source moved, and the `ast` walk cannot. (2) This schema is frozen from the moment it is published, so an enum here would make ADDING A REFUSAL cost a schema version. That is the wrong price on the one action this repository most wants to stay cheap. Naming a refusal is not negotiating with it, and neither is recording one: no refusal changes, moves, gains a route around it or changes exit code because this file exists. */
  "reason": string;
  /** The code the entry path returned, recorded rather than inferred from the reason — `1` (nothing to deliver / the evidence does not cover this tree), `2` (the environment cannot do it, e.g. git has no identity) and `3` (this tree or this branch is unsafe) are different answers, and a reader that re-derived them from the name would be re-implementing a mapping that lives in `deliver.py`. */
  "exit_code": number;
  /** The refusal's own prose, verbatim, scrubbed by the redactor on the way to disk. KEPT rather than replaced by the name: this repository's refusals carry their remedies inside them — `no_git_identity` prints the `git config` commands to run — and dropping the sentence in favour of an enum would be a downgrade wearing a schema. The name is for machines; this is what tells a person what to do. Scrubbed because `.wringer/` is swept by a test that walks every file under it without enumerating write paths, precisely so a new one is covered the day it is added; the console output is unaffected and prints exactly what it printed before this file existed. */
  "message": string;
  /** When the refusal was recorded — local ISO-8601 with offset, to the millisecond, as every other Wringer timestamp. */
  "at": string;
  /** The verification bundle the delivery was reading, where one is known; null otherwise. Null is a real answer and not a placeholder: some refusals fire before any bundle is in hand, and a reader must be able to tell 'no bundle was involved' from 'a bundle was involved and this is which'. */
  "run": string | null;
};

